Capturing a separate (but related) ask for whoever ends up driving this PR forward — coming out of a discussion around #96 (Ollama / OpenAI-compatible endpoint support) and lessons learned from the Cloudflare Workers AI binding work (#bee2). Long comment, sorry; the goal is to make sure this lands in the same config primitive rather than as a separate hook later.

## The user need

Today, `init({ model })` accepts a string keyed off pi-ai's static catalog. Users who want to point at a custom or local OpenAI-compatible endpoint (Ollama, vLLM, LM Studio, llama.cpp, LiteLLM, etc.) hit a wall: the catalog has no entry for their model id, so resolution throws before they ever get to override `baseUrl`.

The cleanest userland answer to #96 isn't "we add native Ollama support" — it's "we expose the same model-resolution extension point Flue uses internally, and you write 8 lines of config to declare your prefix." That extension point should live in `flue.config.ts`.

## What we already do internally for `cloudflare/`

When we shipped Workers AI binding support, we did three things:

1. **Registered a custom pi-ai API** (`'cloudflare-ai-binding'`) via `registerApiProvider`, with our own stream function that dispatches via `env.AI.run()` instead of HTTP.
2. **Built a `Model` literal** for `cloudflare/<id>` strings — `createCloudflareAIBindingModel(id)` returns a `Model<'cloudflare-ai-binding'>` with the suffix as the id, fixed provider name, zeroed metadata.
3. **Branched in `resolveModel`** so `cloudflare/...` strings short-circuit pi-ai's catalog lookup and return the runtime-built Model object instead.

This is currently a hardcoded special case in `internal.ts`. There is no userland equivalent.

## What we'd like out of this PR (eventually)

Two hooks on the config object:

- **`setup()`** — runs once at boot, before any agent handler. Boot-time work: `registerApiProvider`, custom binding setup, anything that needs to happen before pi-ai can dispatch. Most users won't need this; pi-ai's `'openai-completions'` API is already registered as a built-in and covers all OpenAI-compatible endpoints.

- **`models`** — a declarative map of `prefix → factory(suffix) → Model`. Flue's `resolveModel` consults this map *first* (before the existing `cloudflare/` branch and the pi-ai catalog), so a user-defined prefix dispatches to a runtime-built Model just like our `cloudflare/` branch does today.

Sketch:

```ts
// flue.config.ts
import { defineConfig, defineOpenAICompletionsModel } from '@flue/sdk';

export default defineConfig({
  target: 'node',

  // Most users won't need setup() — pi-ai's openai-completions provider is
  // already registered. Reach for it only when registering a brand-new pi-ai
  // API (custom transport, custom binding-style provider, etc.).
  async setup() {
    // e.g. registerApiProvider({ api: 'my-custom-binding', stream, streamSimple })
  },

  models: {
    'ollama/': (id) => defineOpenAICompletionsModel({
      id,
      baseUrl: 'http://localhost:11434/v1',
      provider: 'ollama',
    }),
    'lmstudio/': (id) => defineOpenAICompletionsModel({
      id,
      baseUrl: 'http://localhost:1234/v1',
      provider: 'lmstudio',
    }),
  },
});
```

After this lands: `init({ model: 'ollama/llama3.1:8b' })` works everywhere — top-level, role markdown frontmatter, sub-tasks, compaction. Same for any other OpenAI-compatible endpoint. No catalog gate, no `OPENAI_API_KEY=ollama` workaround, no `ollama cp` aliasing trick. Closes #96 by making the userland answer a real, supported pattern instead of a hack.

## Why a `models` map (declarative) and not a `resolveModel` function (imperative)

We considered both. The map is the better shape for this codebase:

- **Build-time inspectability.** `flue build` can walk the map and emit metadata about what prefixes the project understands. A function body is opaque without evaluating it.
- **Reinforces Flue's existing philosophy.** Runtime behavioral variation lives in agent code (per-call `model` overrides, role inheritance, sub-task model arguments). Config stays declarative. A function hook invites "what if I want regex matching, env-dependent dispatch, fallback chains" — all of which belong in agent code, not config.
- **Better error messages.** "Did you mean `ollama/...`?" suggestions are trivial with a map; expensive with a function.

## Dogfooding ask: rebuild the internal `cloudflare/` integration on this same hook

This is the test that tells us the API is right. If the internal Cloudflare binding integration can be expressed as a `flue.config.ts` that ships with the SDK, then we know the hook surface is sufficient for any real-world custom-provider need.

It maps cleanly:

```ts
// (sketch — what an SDK-shipped config could look like)
import { defineConfig } from '@flue/sdk';
import {
  registerCloudflareAIBindingProvider,
  createCloudflareAIBindingModel,
} from '@flue/sdk/cloudflare';

export default defineConfig({
  setup() {
    registerCloudflareAIBindingProvider();
  },
  models: {
    'cloudflare/': (id) => createCloudflareAIBindingModel(id),
  },
});
```

The hardcoded `cloudflare/` branch in `internal.ts:resolveModel` goes away; the explicit `registerCloudflareAIBindingProvider()` call we just plumbed through the build plugin's generated `_entry.ts` becomes a generic \"call user config's setup()\" call. There's exactly one extension mechanism in the codebase, and Flue's own integrations use it.

The Cloudflare case is a useful stress test because it touches every corner: custom pi-ai API (not openai-completions), custom transport (binding instead of HTTP), AsyncLocalStorage-backed runtime context (env.AI lookup at call time), SSE parsing. If the hook handles that, it'll trivially handle any OpenAI-compatible local endpoint.

## Open design questions for whoever takes this on

Not asks for resolution here, just things to think about:

1. **Async `setup()`?** Probably yes — boot-time registration is sometimes sync (`registerApiProvider`) but config might want to do async work (env-dependent registration, credential fetch). One `await` in the boot path, low cost.

2. **`applyProviderSettings` for user-defined factories.** Flue's existing `init({ providers: { foo: { baseUrl, headers } } })` overrides keyed off provider name. For a Model returned by a user factory, the natural lookup key is `model.provider`. So `init({ providers: { ollama: { baseUrl: '...' } } })` overrides the factory's hardcoded baseUrl. Worth confirming this is the desired ergonomic.

3. **Prefix collision policy.** If both the SDK-shipped config (with `cloudflare/`) and the user's `flue.config.ts` register the same prefix, what wins? Probably user, with a warning — they're explicitly opting into the override. But this needs a clear rule.

4. **Does `setup()` run at build time?** Probably not — it's a runtime concern. But the `models` map needs to be readable at build time enough to know what prefixes are valid (for build-time validation / errors / IDE tooling). Worth keeping that boundary clear.

5. **Resolution order.** Where does the user `models` map sit in the resolution chain relative to the built-in `cloudflare/` branch (assuming dogfooding rebuild doesn't ship in the same PR)? I'd say user-first, then built-ins, then pi-ai catalog. Same answer as collision policy above.

## What this is NOT asking for

- Native Ollama support in core. That's #96, and the answer is "use this hook."
- A `resolveModel` function hook. The map is intentionally more constrained.
- An imperative `flue.registerModelPrefix(...)` API on a passed-in `flue` object inside `setup()`. Side-effectful registration is exactly what we just refactored away from in the Cloudflare auto-import side effect; we don't want to reintroduce that pattern in user space.
- Per-request model behavior variation. Config is for static project-level capabilities; runtime variation lives in agent code.

## TL;DR

When this PR lands, please consider including (or sequencing right after):

- A `models: Record<prefix, (suffix) => Model>` field on the config object.
- A `setup(): void | Promise<void>` hook for boot-time `registerApiProvider`-style work.
- A `defineOpenAICompletionsModel(...)` (or similarly named) helper export from `@flue/sdk` that fills sensible zero-defaults so users don't have to construct a full pi-ai `Model` literal by hand.
- Migration of the internal `cloudflare/` branch in `resolveModel` onto this same hook (dogfood test).

We're not married to any specific implementation here — this PR will probably go through a few iterations before settling. The above is the user-need-and-shape of what we want the config primitive to enable, so that #96 and similar future asks (custom endpoints, custom bindings, custom prefixes) have a single supported answer instead of growing into a pile of native-support-for-X feature requests.

